# PitchEdge — Cursor Project Rules

You are helping build PitchEdge, a publicly-calibrated World Cup prediction
engine and content product. Read docs/PRD.md and docs/ARCHITECTURE.md before
generating code. When a task is ambiguous, re-read those docs rather than
guessing.

## Prime directives
- Calibration over accuracy. We optimize and report proper scoring rules
  (Brier, log loss), never win-rate vanity metrics.
- The match_predictions table is APPEND-ONLY and IMMUTABLE after kickoff. Never
  write code that updates or deletes a logged prediction. Guard every prediction
  insert so predicted_utc < kickoff_utc, else raise.
- This is an analytics/content product, NOT a betting product. Never write code
  that places, brokers, or facilitates real-money wagers, manages a betting
  bankroll, or integrates a sportsbook account.

## Stack & conventions
- Python 3.12, dependency management with `uv` (pyproject.toml). No global pip.
- Package code lives under `src/pitchedge/`. Tests under `tests/` mirroring the
  package layout. One responsibility per module.
- Postgres 16 via Docker Compose. DB access through a single `db.py` layer;
  no raw connection strings scattered across modules. Use SQLAlchemy core or
  psycopg with parameterized queries — never string-format SQL.
- Config (API keys, DB url, Telegram token) via environment variables loaded
  from a `.env` that is gitignored. Never hardcode secrets. Provide `.env.example`.
- All randomness takes an explicit seed argument; simulations must be
  reproducible. Default seed lives in config.
- Type hints on all public functions. Docstrings stating units (probabilities in
  [0,1], goals as expected counts, times in UTC).
- Times are UTC everywhere internally; convert only at the display edge.

## Modeling rules
- Probabilities for a match must sum to 1 (assert/renormalize). Never return
  un-normalized vectors past the model boundary.
- De-vig before using any market odds as a probability.
- The blend weight `w` is a config value chosen by backtest, not a magic number
  inline. Document its provenance.
- For low-data teams, shrink Dixon-Coles params toward the Elo/confederation
  prior. Flag (in logs) any team with < N qualifying historical matches.

## Quality bar
- Every model and sim module ships with pytest tests, including at least one
  property test (e.g., probabilities sum to 1; sim advancement probs per group
  sum to ~4 expected advancers within tolerance).
- Pure functions where possible; isolate I/O (DB, network) behind thin adapters
  so the math is unit-testable without a database.
- Prefer clear, explicit code over cleverness. No premature abstraction.
- When you finish a task, state which validation steps from docs/BUILD_PLAN.md
  the user should run, and stop. Do not start the next phase unprompted.

## Things to avoid
- Do not invent data source endpoints. If an API's exact schema is unknown,
  write an adapter with a clearly-marked TODO and a fixture-based test, and tell
  the user what to confirm.
- Do not claim the model beats the market in any code comment, log, or UI string.
- No emojis in code. Keep log output structured and parseable.
